# The starting cast. One edge per line - this file replaces the `fact` clause.
#
# Every capitalised word is a NAME, not a variable: the loader matches each line
# against the edge types declared in story.graphwrite, and each distinct name it
# finds in a `?` position gets a node id. The same name anywhere in this file is
# always the same node, which is what makes the graph connect up.
#
# A bare NUMBER in a `?` position is a YEAR. The story opens in the first week
# of the year 1000, so `Aldric born in 962` makes him thirty-eight, and the age
# guards (`olderThan`) read that straight off the graph. Two people born in one
# year share the week node; that is fine, nothing matches two birth weeks at once.
#
# Blank lines and # comments are skipped. A line that matches no declared pattern
# is an error rather than a silently ignored edge - a typo here is otherwise
# invisible until a rule mysteriously never fires.
#
# Someone who is grown and single needs `is unmarried` AND `is unattached`; a
# woman who may bear needs `is fertile`. Someone who already likes another must
# NOT be unattached, and needs a `smitten on` year in the past.

# --- Wealdholt ---------------------------------------------------------------

StMaerwyn is a church in Wealdholt

# The priest. He belongs to no house and takes no wife, so the match and
# courtship rules pass him by, and he is given no `keeps the peace`, which is
# what a rule consumes to hatch a plot - so he cannot start one. He can still
# be drawn into somebody else's as an accomplice, and share the guilt, and
# have to take that to the other parish himself. That is deliberate.

Father_Oswin serves StMaerwyn
Father_Oswin is man
Father_Oswin is alive
Father_Oswin born in 958
Father_Oswin is grown
Father_Oswin lives in Wealdholt

